{
  "person_before": "Owen Price is a supermarket night worker in his twenties who takes indoor bouldering far too seriously. Before the arrival, his concerns are finishing his shift, avoiding Leanne’s disapproval and reaching the climbing wall before it becomes crowded. Climbing has taught him balance, foot placement and how to work through a difficult movement, not exceptional strength or fearlessness.",
  "exception": "Everyone receives exactly one Slot, but Owen alone has unlimited capacity within his Slot. He can install every skill he obtains without releasing any existing skill. Other people must relinquish their installed skill to replace it; their memories and mundane expertise remain, but reacquiring the abandoned magic requires another source. Owen still needs sources, practice, stamina and attention. His retained skills create ever more opportunities for genuinely new demonstrations and combinations, letting him advance faster than nearby single-skill users without a hidden multiplier.",
  "first_use": "In Chapter 1, scenes 1–4, Owen compares his unlimited-capacity sheet with Leanne’s single-capacity sheet, then chooses Cling to reach Dev across the damaged loading corridor. Its successful use advances him from Slot Rank 1 to 2. When the return wall breaks apart, he takes an Air Step seed from a cultivated-looking root without losing Cling. After a failed attempt and recoverable practice, he combines adhesion, an air foothold and a wall catch to carry a rescue line across the gap, reaching Rank 3. Leanne anchors the safe end, Owen secures the line to surviving structure, and the staff help Dev across. His exception first matters because both installed skills remain usable during the same rescue.",
  "want": "First, Owen wants to get his coworkers out of the broken shop. Once they are safe, he wants to reach the inhabited cliff orchard for help, water, food and another skill—and because he cannot stop imagining the heights he could explore. Shared work with its inhabitants develops that personal excitement into a desire to make extraordinary places accessible to others without destroying the homes and cultivation that make those journeys possible.",
  "system": {
    "name": "The Slot System",
    "manner": "The arrival message appears worldwide on active screens and directly in human vision: EVERY PERSON HAS RECEIVED ONE SLOT. CHOOSE WHAT YOU WILL BE ABLE TO DO. Personal sheets silently report Slot count, capacity, installed skills and Slot Rank, independently of anyone’s approval. Touching an intact skill source identifies its permission before commitment; taking the skill consumes the source. Installation earns no advancement. Demonstrated control under real environmental demand, materially harder applications and genuinely new combinations earn progress. Easy repetition eventually stops contributing, although practice prepares harder feats. Higher ranks strengthen and extend installed abilities without adding unrelated magical functions. There are no classes granting extra skills.",
    "look": "Still white lettering appears against a faint charcoal field in Owen’s vision, echoed by the interrupted displays during arrival. His first sheet reads: OWEN PRICE; SLOT COUNT: 1; SLOT CAPACITY: UNLIMITED; INSTALLED SKILLS: NONE; SLOT RANK: 1. Later changes update the relevant lines without a speaking guide or celebratory spectacle.",
    "steps": 8,
    "strongest_known": "The highest locally attested practitioner is a Vey seasonal-bridge keeper at counted Rank 8. Their sole skill, Threadline, can sustain temporary cords across a broad ravine through prolonged bridge work. This is the strongest rank Owen learns about, not proof of a final ceiling or worldwide maximum.",
    "what_rising_gives": "Cling gains the strength and endurance to hold through harder loads and violent weather. Air Step provides a stronger brief foothold, supporting more demanding crossings while still requiring recovery of breath before reuse. Threadline gains tensile strength and duration, making dependable temporary routes possible with learned control. Additional functions require additional skills: Owen’s eventual interest in Load Share concerns spreading weight across his lines, not a function that rank alone grants.",
    "start_rank": 1
  },
  "threat": {
    "what": "Earth remains recognisable but is intersected by cultivated cliff terrain whose broken roots, rain channels and routes threaten access to shelter, food and water. The Vey’s damaged irrigation and separated households intersect with human survival needs. An older dispute over root-borne blight, frightened assumptions about invasion and human removal of skill-seeds complicate cooperation. Owen can reach places his companions cannot, but personal mobility alone cannot produce a safe communal route.",
    "first_reach": "In Chapter 1, scene 1, a root tears through the supermarket floor, tilts the loading corridor and cuts off the staff exit. Dev is stranded beyond the widening break. The danger becomes more than an outward crossing when Owen reaches him and discovers that the return wall has pulled apart."
  },
  "turn": {
    "event": "In Chapter 2, scenes 5–8, scouting for usable water brings Owen to a diverted orchard gate, fresh tool marks and Serri, a frightened Vey looking for a missing relative. The terraces become someone’s damaged home rather than a supply of ownerless discoveries. Through Chapter 3, scenes 9–12, repairing the channel and negotiating access connect his desire for new skills to a reciprocal relationship with people whose own survival and family ties matter.",
    "when": "inside the first arc"
  },
  "second_system": null,
  "first_arc": {
    "opens": "Chapter 1, scenes 1–4, delivers one connected escape and its aftermath. Scene 1 establishes Owen’s ordinary shift, the worldwide arrival, contrasting personal sheets and Dev’s isolation. Scene 2 gives the consequential choice of Cling over Anchor or Kindle, Owen’s imperfect but successful crossing and Rank 2; Leanne uses Anchor to hold a rolling stock cage. Scene 3 introduces the graft sleeve and Air Step seed, proves that Owen retains both skills, and rewards the successful rescue-line combination with Rank 3. Scene 4 completes the physically demanding evacuation. Dev needs treatment, Owen’s forearms ache, and Leanne questions whether the consumed seed was someone’s crop. Her sheet remains at Rank 2. Owen explains his capacity and offers to scout but refuses an immediate exhausted climb. Lanterns, terraces and a built rain channel draw his attention toward the inhabited heights.",
    "middle": "Chapter 2, scenes 5–8, makes the surviving supermarket stock the practical basis of a refuge: people treat injuries, check food and seek water without waiting for official designation. Owen and Leanne investigate the elevated channel when the taps fail. His movement skills provide access, not irrigation expertise; Leanne’s increasingly demanding Anchor applications establish meaningful specialist growth. Pruned branches, ceramic graft sleeves, sleeping hollows and low handrails lead to Serri and evidence of interrupted maintenance. Chapter 3, scenes 9–12, develops contact through gestures and shared work rather than instant translation. Repairing the channel earns a limited water-sharing agreement. Serri offers a cultivated Threadline seed for help reaching a stranded nursery whose grafts are drying. Owen installs it immediately but must learn to keep its temporary cords between touched surfaces dependable when his attention shifts. Chapter 4, scenes 13–16, tests his combined movement and lines while Serri identifies safe grafts and dangerous roots. Owen’s advancement pulls ahead, but a failed line drops supplies onto a lower terrace and forces a lengthy retrieval. His elegant personal route is unsuitable for people carrying water and children. He spends time helping build a slower, partly mundane route; nobody loses ranks or skills. Chapter 5, scenes 17–20, reaches a deliberately sealed boundary. Family marks, cut bridges and newer repair knots reveal the Vey dispute over connections severed after blight crossed a shared bridge. Serri’s relatives seek reunion, another community fears infection, and human scavengers are taking cultivated seeds. Investigating with a local cultivator reveals an active diseased root beyond the seal. Owen chooses to spend his time protecting the orchard rather than stripping it, without claiming authority over whether its inhabitants should reopen their boundary.",
    "closes": "Chapter 6, scenes 21–24, brings a storm whose runoff threatens both nursery and supermarket through the damaged boundary. Owen’s combined skills let him carry help and connections between isolated work parties; Leanne’s stronger Anchor and Vey specialists handle loads he cannot. Their cooperation produces a working diversion and a traversable evacuation route that preserves access between the heights and the shop, not a cure for blight or an end to the apocalypse. Owen continues earning counted advancement through harder uses and new combinations, retaining every earned skill. He has seen Load Share’s grafted source in the nursery and now wants its ability to distribute weight across his temporary lines, while understanding why its cultivators value it. The arc ends with a usable route, developing relationships and an inviting next pursuit. The System’s purpose, the cause of Owen’s exception and the larger geography remain unresolved."
  },
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "subject": "Owen’s exception",
      "question": "Will unlimited capacity actually let Owen retain Cling while using another skill to make an otherwise impossible rescue route?",
      "answered_by_scene": 3
    },
    {
      "subject": "The inhabited orchard",
      "question": "Can Owen establish reciprocal contact with the orchard’s people rather than treating their water and skill-seeds as ownerless supplies?",
      "answered_by_scene": 12
    },
    {
      "subject": "The sealed routes",
      "question": "Why were the orchard bridges deliberately cut, and does the danger behind those closures still exist?",
      "answered_by_scene": 20
    },
    {
      "subject": "Shared access",
      "question": "Can Owen turn his exceptional personal mobility into a route that other people can actually use, with help from specialists whose strengths exceed his own?",
      "answered_by_scene": 24
    }
  ]
}

